# Contract-driven development

Definition: `standards/contract/philosophy.md`. Drafts live in `standards/contract/proposals/`. Feature `.mdc` files live under `.cursor/rules/dl_toolbox_structure/` mirroring `src/dl_toolbox/`. Do not add a per-feature `philosophy.md`. Drafts never modify `.mdc` files.

A proposal is one of the three shapes below. Do not mix them. If a later call site still re-checks the contract's assumptions, the contract is not in use. If implementation needs a default, a `None`, or a new slot, stop and revise the `.mdc` and the philosophy note. When a real contract is added, update the tree in `.cursor/rules/dl_toolbox_structure/index.mdc` and the keyword `description` on `DL_projects/.cursor/rules/dl_toolbox_structure.mdc`.

## 1. Entity (Python object)

Reference: Image — `.cursor/rules/dl_toolbox_structure/base/input_output/images.mdc`

The itch is a helper (`cv2.imread`, `process_path`). The contract is the object the code can hold. Mark `CONTRACT` when these six exist in code:

1. Entity = native payload + spec
2. Spec = product of properties (illegal combinations rejected)
3. Validation owned once (spec consistency + payload match)
4. Named sources
5. Owned parse APIs (required args = what that source cannot supply)
6. Named convert maps (or an explicit error)

## 2. Functionality (class)

Reference: MLflow logger — `.cursor/rules/dl_toolbox_structure/dl_engine/loggers/mlflow.mdc`

The entity is external state (a store, a run). There is nothing to wrap. Specify the official behaviors, the algorithm of each (assumed state, the operation, state afterward), then translate into a class. Mark `CONTRACT` when the class in code is that translation.

